  You told me not to ask questions, so I'm skipping the clarifying round and locking in assumptions explicitly. Here's
  the design:

  [1mAssumptions (POC-grade, stated so you can veto them)[0m
  - [1mZero new dependencies.[0m JWT is HS256 signed with [38;5;153mnode:crypto[39m HMAC (~20 lines), passwords hashed with [38;5;153mcrypto.scrypt[39m +
    per-user salt. Avoids [38;5;153mjsonwebtoken[39m/[38;5;153mbcrypt[39m installs and keeps the POC self-contained.
  - [1mMock email = stdout.[0m The "mailer" [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms the reset link. No SMTP, no provider.
  - [1mEverything in memory.[0m Users in the existing [38;5;153musers[39m array (wrapped in a tiny store module), reset tokens in a [38;5;153mMap[39m.
    Restart wipes state.
  - [1mSecret[0m from [38;5;153mprocess.env.JWT_SECRET[39m, falling back to a fixed dev constant with a stdout warning.

  [1mShape[0m
  app.js              mounts the auth router, exports app, listens only when run directly
  auth/store.js       user CRUD over the in-memory array
  auth/passwords.js   scrypt hash + constant-time verify
  auth/jwt.js         sign/verify HS256, 24h exp
  auth/mailer.js      mock send -> stdout
  auth/middleware.js  requireAuth: Bearer token -> req.user, else 401
  auth/routes.js      the five endpoints

  [1mEndpoints:[0m [38;5;153mPOST /auth/register[39m, [38;5;153mPOST /auth/login[39m, [38;5;153mGET /me[39m (protected), [38;5;153mPOST /auth/forgot-password[39m (always 202, link to
  stdout), [38;5;153mPOST /auth/reset-password[39m (single-use token, 15 min TTL).

  [1mTooling:[0m built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runner, no linter — cheapest thing that still proves the flows work end to end.

  [1mKnown POC limits:[0m no rate limiting, no refresh tokens, no token revocation on password reset, secret in env only.

